---
name: fix-code-comments
description: Write and fix code comments so a reader with zero context understands them. Apply when writing any comment or docstring, and when asked to clean up comments.
metadata:
  trigger: Writing or editing code comments, docstrings, or test docstrings
---

# Fix Code Comments

Every comment must pass one test. **The reader has this file open and nothing else.** They did not read the PR. They did not sit in the design review. They do not know the team's shorthand. They joined last week.

Load the `ste-writing` skill rules for sentence form. This skill controls the content.

## The zero-context test

Before you finish any change, reread every comment you wrote or touched. For each noun phrase, ask three questions:

1. **Which one?** "The check", "the callback", "the cleanup step", "the race" name nothing. Name the exact thing: `close_stale_agent_shift`, "the `disconnect_agent` callback".
2. **Defined where?** A term is legal only when it is (a) a code identifier, spelled exactly as in the code, (b) defined in the same comment, or (c) defined once in the class or module docstring that the reader will see first.
3. **Would a new hire say it?** If the word came from the team's heads and not from the code on screen, replace it with plain words.

If any answer lives only in your head, in the PR, or in the chat, the comment is broken. Rewrite it.

## Banned vocabulary

Do not use these words in comments unless the same sentence defines them with a real identifier. This list grows; the rule behind it is question 3 above.

| Banned | Write instead |
|---|---|
| liveness | proof that the agent (user, worker) is still here |
| watermark | the last time we saw X (`last_seen_at`) |
| demote, demotion | set the status to offline |
| dangling, stale (in prose) | left over from a crash; never closed |
| close-check, health-check (coined names) | the delayed check (`close_stale_agent_shift`) |
| poison (a transaction) | break the whole outer transaction |
| touch (in prose) | update `last_seen_at` |
| self-heal | open a shift again so the report shows the agent |
| routable | still receives new calls |
| grace period (undefined) | define it once at the constant, then reference the constant |

Identifiers keep their names. A method named `_close_stale` may be called by name. The prose around it still explains in plain words.

## Coined terms: define once, by their real name

A system sometimes needs a short name for a mechanism. Then define it exactly once, in the class or module docstring, and bind it to the real artifact:

> We schedule the `close_stale_agent_shift` task to run `AGENT_SHIFT_GRACE_SECONDS` later. That task, called "the delayed check" in the comments below, closes the shift only when the agent is still gone.

After that, "the delayed check" is legal in this file. Never coin a term in an inline comment.

## Mechanism words need their consequence

`savepoint`, `on_commit`, `select_for_update`, `IntegrityError` may appear only next to a plain-words sentence that says what happens to the reader's world:

Bad:
```python
# Savepoint: a lost one-active-per-agent constraint race must
# not poison the outer transaction.
```

Good:
```python
# Two callers can try to create the shift at the same time. The
# database allows one active shift per agent, so the loser gets
# an IntegrityError. The inner transaction.atomic() keeps that
# error from breaking the whole outer transaction.
```

## More real before/after pairs

Bad (assumes review context, four coined terms in one docstring):
```python
"""Grace-period check. Closes the shift when nothing proved liveness
since the event that scheduled it. For an agent whose availability is
already off (a registration reset), the shift ends at its watermark."""
```

Good:
```python
"""The delayed check: is the agent really gone?

The ``close_stale_agent_shift`` task calls this one grace period
after a disconnect, a shift open, or a login reset. It closes the
shift only when nothing shows that the agent is still here."""
```

Bad ("a cleanup step" — which step?):
```python
# Status online with no shift: a cleanup step was lost.
```

Good:
```python
# The agent's status is online but no shift exists. A crash cleanup
# half-finished: it closed the shift and then failed to reset the
# status. Open a shift so the report shows this agent again.
```

## Existing rules still apply

The Code Comments section in your global agent rules file (`CLAUDE.md` or `AGENTS.md`) still governs what to write about: state the file as it is now, never describe a diff, an absence, or a former state. This skill adds how to say it: ELI5, explicit, zero context assumed.

## Procedure when asked to fix comments in a file

1. Read the whole file.
2. List every comment and docstring.
3. Run the zero-context test on each noun phrase.
4. Rewrite failures. Prefer everyday words. Keep sentences short (STE).
5. When a comment leans on a coined term used more than once, add the one-time definition to the class or module docstring and bind it to the real identifier.
6. Check helper names too. A method named after jargon (`_demote_unavailable`) spreads the jargon to every call site; rename it (`_set_offline_if_still_gone`) when the rename is cheap.
7. Reread the file once more as the new hire. Every "which one?" must have an answer on screen.
"}}

